[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L3-UC
TITLE: Use cases

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
A use case is a functionality the element provides to a user. Exactly one main
scenario plus any number of alternative scenarios.

**Every step is one of four kinds**, recorded in `STEP_TYPE`:

| `STEP_TYPE` | What it is | Must name |
|---|---|---|
| `User-interaction` | User interacts with a user interface | the `UI-` and the data entered or shown |
| `Function-call` | Invokes internal logic | the `TF-`, its input, and what happens to its output |
| `Outbound-call` | Technical interaction with another element | the `TO-` — but see below |
| `Activity` | Internal processing by the element | nothing external |

**Prefer function calls over outbound calls.** Most logic, including calls to
external interfaces, should be encapsulated in a technical function. A direct
`Outbound-call` step is justified only where creating a function would be
unnecessary overhead, which is rare. When unsure, use a function call - it reads
better and keeps error handling in one place.

**Where a called function fails, the error is handled in that function's
specification**, not here. Alternative scenarios at this level cover failures
the *use case* must respond to.
<<<

