[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L3-E
TITLE: Entities

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The entities this element **stores**. For complex data structures, include a
diagram.

```mermaid
erDiagram
  ENTITY_A ||--o{ ENTITY_B : "TBD"
```
<<<

[ENTITY]
UID: E-01
STATUS: Draft
PERSISTENCE: Persistent
TITLE: TBD
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - What this entity represents and what it stores, and its purpose within
this element. One or two sentences.
<<<
ATTRIBUTES: >>>
| ID | Attribute | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| E-01.1 | TBD | TBD | required | TBD |
| E-01.2 | TBD | TBD | optional | TBD |

**Naming:** camelCase - `userId`, `createdAt`, `orderTotal`.

**Types:** be specific. Primitives `string` `integer` `decimal` `boolean`
`datetime` `date` `time`; collections `array` `object`; special `enum` `uuid`
`url` `email` `phoneNumber`; references to another entity by its ID.

**Required vs optional:** `required` cannot be null or empty; `optional` can.

**Descriptions:** what it stores, any constraints (min/max length, range,
format), references to other entities, and whether the value is derived.

The `E-01.1` attribute IDs are referenced from technical function steps. They
are table content rather than node identifiers, so they are not link-checked -
keep them consistent by hand.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: BE-01
  ROLE: Refines

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L3-QR
TITLE: Quality requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
How well the element performs its functions. Set `ELEMENT_SPECIFIC: Yes` where
the requirement is a purely element-level technical goal with no system-level
counterpart - the template notes this is legitimate.
<<<

[QUALITY_REQUIREMENT]
UID: QR-01
QUALITY_CATEGORY: Performance
OBLIGATION: shall
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Draft
VERIFICATION: Test
ELEMENT_SPECIFIC: No
TITLE: TBD
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - What aspect of quality is specified and why it matters.

Categories: `Performance` response times, throughput, resource usage;
`Scalability` handling growing workload; `Availability` uptime, reliability,
fault tolerance; `Security` authentication, authorisation, data protection;
`Usability` ease of use, learnability, accessibility; `Maintainability` code
quality, documentation, testability; `Compatibility` platform, browser,
integration support; `Data-integrity` consistency, accuracy, validation;
`Compliance` regulatory and standards adherence.
<<<
